course: biology
lesson: 193
title: How a dog thinks: a world of smells, sounds and faces
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: On a walk your dog stops at a lamp post and sniffs for ages while you tug the lead. For the dog this is reading the news: who passed by, how long ago, and whether it was a male or a female.
explain: A dog lives first in a world of smells, then of sounds; its eyes matter less. It sniffs in short quick bursts that carry smells to a special part of its nose, and it can even tell which nostril a smell came into.
explain: Its ears catch sounds too high or too quiet for us, which is why it runs to the door when the family car is still far down the road.
explain: Dogs have also spent thousands of years living with people, and they became experts at our faces, voices and pointing fingers, better at this one thing than chimpanzees.
explain: Some dog behaviour looks human but is not: the famous guilty look after the bin is knocked over is mostly a reaction to your cross voice.
fact: Other dogs leave scent marks with urine (wee) on lamp posts that tell who passed, how long ago, and whether they were male or female.
fact: A dog's nose has hundreds of millions of smell sensors and smells many thousands of times better than ours, so it can find a biscuit inside a closed bag in a backpack.
fact: Dogs sniff in short bursts that carry smells to a special part of the nose, and they can tell which nostril a smell entered, which helps them find where it comes from.
fact: A warm dry nose does not always mean a dog is ill; that is an old myth.
fact: Dogs learn the sound of each car engine, and they hear a dog whistle that is too high for people to hear.
fact: Dogs see blue and yellow well, but red and green look greyish to them, so a blue or yellow ball is easier to find on a lawn.
fact: Dogs learn daily routines and may notice how a person's smell fades in the house during the day, which may help them seem to know when someone is coming home.
fact: Dogs get stressed when a routine suddenly changes, for example when the holidays end.
fact: Dogs follow human pointing better than chimpanzees do, and puppies can follow pointing very young.
fact: Some border collies have learned the names of over a thousand toys, but most dogs understand tone of voice more than exact words.
fact: Scientists found that dogs make the same guilty look whether or not they did anything, if the owner is cross; the look is a way to calm an upset person.
fact: Dogs have the same sleep stages as us, including the dreaming stage, and puppies and old dogs dream more often than adult dogs.
fact: Many dogs nibble grass and it is usually normal; scientists are not fully sure why, and wolves eat some plants too.
exercise: Why does a dog sniff a lamp post for so long? | It is reading scent marks about who passed and when | It is looking for food stuck to the post | It is trying to clean the post | It is resting its legs
exercise: What can a dog learn from a scent mark? | Who left it, about how long ago, and whether male or female | Only the colour and the size of the other dog | Nothing at all, it just likes the smell and wants to roll in it | The name of the other dog's owner and the street where they live
exercise: How do dogs sniff to smell best? | In short quick bursts | In one very long deep breath | Through their mouths only | By holding their breath
exercise: How does knowing which nostril a smell came into help a dog? | It helps it work out where the smell comes from | It helps it sneeze less | It tells it how hot or how cold the smell is today | It helps it see further and more clearly in the dark
exercise: A dog's nose is warm and dry after a nap. What does that mean? | Not necessarily anything; a dry nose is not a sure sign of illness | The dog is certainly ill and must see a vet at once | The dog has stopped smelling | The dog has a fever for sure, because healthy noses are always cold and wet
exercise: How can a dog know that it is the family car coming, not a neighbour's? | It learns the sound of each engine | It reads the number plate | It smells petrol from miles away | It guesses every time
exercise: Why can a dog hear a dog whistle when you cannot? | Dogs hear much higher sounds than people | The whistle is only quiet for people | Dogs feel the whistle with their paws | Dogs see the sound waves
exercise: Which ball colour is easiest for a dog to spot on green grass? | Blue or yellow | Red | Green | Orange
exercise: How might a dog seem to know when school pick-up time is near? | It learns the routine and may notice smells fading through the day | It can read the clock on the kitchen wall | It hears the school bell ring from home, however far away the school is | It is told by the other dogs in the street, who bark the time to each other
exercise: Why might a dog be stressed when the summer holidays end? | Its daily routine suddenly changes | It misses the hot weather | It hates school uniforms | It dislikes autumn leaves
exercise: You point at a treat. What does a dog usually do? | Follows your pointing to the treat | Sniffs your finger | Stares at the ceiling | Walks away
exercise: How many toy names have some border collies learned? | Over a thousand | About five | Exactly twenty | None, dogs cannot learn names
exercise: What do most dogs understand best when we speak? | Our tone of voice | Every word exactly | Long sentences | Spelled-out words
exercise: Why does a dog look guilty after the bin was knocked over? | It is reacting to its owner's cross voice and face | It remembers what it did and feels sorry, just like a person | It is ill | It wants more rubbish from the bin, so it is asking politely
exercise: What is a sleeping dog probably doing when its paws twitch and it woofs softly? | Dreaming | Having a fit every time | Pretending to sleep | Running a fever
exercise: Which dogs dream most often? | Puppies and old dogs | Only big dogs | Only adult dogs | Only dogs that eat grass
exercise: Your dog nibbles grass in the garden. What is true? | It is usually normal, though scientists are not sure why | It is always very ill and needs medicine straight away | It thinks it is a cow | It is trying to brush its teeth, because grass cleans them like a toothbrush
test: «hurry up Rex, it's just a lamppost ffs» What is Rex really doing? | Reading smells left by other dogs, like a newspaper | Admiring the lamp | Looking for a bone it lost there and buried last week | Waiting for the light to turn on so it can see where to go
test: Count them: three dogs sniff a lamp post. How many nostrils are at work? | 6 | 3 | 9 | 12
test: «the dog went mad at my backpack and the biscuit was in a sealed bag inside, how???» What explains it? | A dog smells many thousands of times better than we do | The bag was see-through to dogs, because dogs see through plastic | It heard the biscuit | It was a lucky guess, because dogs check every bag just in case
test: «Rocky's nose is all dry and warm, he's sick, call the vet now!!» Is a dry nose alone a sure sign of illness? | No, it is an old myth; look at how he eats, drinks and acts | Yes, always call the vet at once, a dry nose means trouble | Yes, a dry nose means he has a high fever and must stay in bed | No, a dog's nose is always dry
test: What would happen if you threw a red ball and a yellow ball onto a green lawn for your dog? | It would probably spot the yellow ball more easily | It would see the red one first, because red is the brightest colour | It would see both balls equally well | It would see neither ball at all
test: «mum, the dog's already at the door and I can't even hear the car» Why? | Dogs hear quieter sounds and know the sound of our engine | The dog is psychic | It smelled mum coming through the walls and the closed windows | The dog just sits there by the door all day long, every single day
test: A chimpanzee and a dog watch you point at one of two cups. Which is usually better at following the point? | The dog | The chimpanzee | Neither can follow pointing | Both always guess wrong
test: «that collie knows 1000 toys, so my dog must understand every word I say» What is closer to the truth? | Most dogs understand tone of voice better than exact words | Yes, all dogs know every word, they just choose not to listen | No dog can learn any word | Only collies can hear words at all; other dogs just hear noise
test: Your brother shouts at the dog over a mess the cat made, and the dog puts its ears back and looks away. What does this show? | The dog's look reacts to the shouting, not to guilt | The dog did the mess and remembers doing it | The dog is laughing at your brother and trying to hide it | The dog is about to bite
test: In a cartoon a dog reads a newspaper and checks a wristwatch. What is true of real dogs? | They read scents instead and keep time through routines | They can read words if taught for long enough by a patient owner | They wear watches in the wild | They have no sense of routine at all
test: Count them: a dog's dream makes all four paws twitch three times each. How many twitches is that? | 12 | 7 | 4 | 16
exam: Identify the principal sense by means of which the domestic dog apprehends its surroundings. | The sense of smell | The sense of sight | The sense of taste | The sense of touch
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the so-called guilty look of the dog. | It is principally a response to the displeasure of the owner rather than a recollection of wrongdoing | It constitutes clear proof that the dog recalls its misdeed and experiences sincere remorse on account of it | It occurs only when the dog is innocent | It occurs only in the presence of other dogs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the colour vision of the dog with respect to a red object upon green grass? | The object appears greyish and is difficult to distinguish | The object appears more vivid than to a human | The object is invisible in all conditions | The object appears blue
exam: Ascertain the significance of a warm and dry nose in a dog which is otherwise behaving normally. | It is not in itself a reliable indication of illness | It is a certain indication of fever, requiring the immediate attention of a veterinary surgeon | It is a certain indication of blindness | It indicates the loss of the sense of smell
